title: Black October / AI Developer Platforms
page: breaches/black-october-ai-platforms.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: At least nine AI developer platforms reported security incidents in October 2026, and the recurring target was stored environment variables, especially keys that grant access to AI models.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 9 | AI developer platforms reported incidents, October 6 to 30 | At least nine; Wiz gathered them (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz)
stat: 3,218,965 | environment variable records read at Zeabur | Confirmed, along with 321,810 accounts
Separately, two stolen Bedrock keys carried about 2.5 million calls, likely over $100,000 at list price, per Wiz. The threat actor is unattributed; no single actor is established across the nine.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

Between October 6 and October 30, 2026, at least nine AI developer platforms reported security incidents: Fireworks AI, Andromeda, Zeabur, Lightning AI, OpenCode, Railway, Warp, CommandCode and Northflank. Wiz gathered them in a report titled Black October, and has since appended five more reported incidents.

At Zeabur, the attacker read 3,218,965 environment variable records and 321,810 accounts from the platform database, both confirmed. Separately, two stolen Amazon Bedrock keys carried about 2.5 million calls, which Wiz says likely ran up over $100,000 at list price.

Say the disclosure aloud as well. The page draws heavily on research published by Wiz, and Wiz also responded to the Northflank incident. The page also cites research by Google's threat intelligence group, and Google acquired Wiz in March 2026. The site's author works at Wiz. Every source is linked so you can read it directly.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: October 6 to 30 | At least nine AI developer platforms report security incidents
event: October 14 | Zeabur: an AWS key and the admin token in use; database read October 15
event: October 20 | Railway's attack begins, it believes, running until October 22
event: October 30 | Northflank: first known decryption, more than eight hours before Wiz's alert
event: November 1 | Trigger.dev discloses its incident, one of five Wiz has since added
event: December 31 | Zeabur will shut down, which it calls an operational decision
All dates are 2026. The two Bedrock abuse cases have no calendar dates on the page. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page's dates, in order. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

Between October 6 and October 30, at least nine AI developer platforms reported incidents. At Zeabur, an AWS key and the cluster admin token were in use within four minutes on October 14, and the platform database was read on October 15. Railway believes its attack ran from October 20 until October 22. On October 30 Northflank told customers to assume all environment variables were decrypted, and its November 6 update dates the first known decryption of secrets to between 02:39 and 03:08 UTC on October 30, more than eight hours before Wiz's alert. Trigger.dev disclosed its incident on November 1, and it is one of five reported incidents Wiz has since appended. Zeabur will shut down on December 31, 2026, which it calls an operational decision.

The Bedrock abuse cases are timed only against the stolen keys: within 30 minutes of the reported credential access, then four and seven days after validation. The page gives them no calendar dates, so do not place them on this line.

== Two halves, not one attacker
layout: compare
col: The platform half | Stages 01 to 05: Zeabur, whose preliminary report is the fullest public record | Stage 06: the other platforms' reports | Wiz says no single vulnerability or dependency links them; most are still under analysis
col: The downstream half | Stages 07 to 09: two Amazon Bedrock abuse cases | Wiz ties them to one of these vendor breaches, unnamed | Wiz establishes no direct link from its indicators to the wider incidents
This page is a composite, and the two halves should not be read as one attacker.
notes:
The page is careful to say what kind of record it is, and this deck keeps that framing.

The platform half follows Zeabur, whose preliminary report is the fullest public record, through stages 01 to 05. Stage 06 covers what the other platforms have reported. Wiz says no single vulnerability or dependency links the nine incidents, and most are still under analysis. The threat actor is unattributed.

The downstream half follows two Amazon Bedrock abuse cases that Wiz attributes to one of these vendor breaches without naming it. The page does not say which breach the stolen keys in stages 07 to 09 came from. Stage 09 returns to this: Zeabur later named an address from one of the operations that checked the keys in its own compromise, and the page says that overlap does not show Zeabur's keys are the ones in these cases.

Wiz does not establish a direct link between its listed indicators and the wider Black October incidents. When you present the two halves, say plainly that they should not be read as one attacker.

== The platform half on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Credentials left behind | T1552.001
step: 02 | Initial Access | Public admin interface | T1078.004
step: 03 | Credential Access | Every cluster service | T1552.007
step: 04 | Lateral Movement | Root passwords | T1021
step: 05 | Collection | The platform database | T1213
step: 06 | Credential Access | Same objective elsewhere | T1552
Stages 01 to 05 follow Zeabur; stage 04 is a second path, through Google Cloud. Arrows show stage order only.
notes:
Here is the platform half on one slide: stages 01 to 06, each with its phase and the ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stages 01 and 02 are initial access at Zeabur: credentials left in a private repository after an August rotation missed them, then a cluster admin token used through an admin interface reachable from the public internet. Stage 03, credential access, is the read of service settings and secrets across the shared cluster. Stage 04, lateral movement, is a second path, through Google Cloud, where plaintext root passwords opened dedicated servers. The two paths met at stage 05, collection, when the platform database was read.

Stage 06 leaves Zeabur. It sets out what the other platforms reported: different doors and the same prize.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order. Stage 04 is what the page calls a second path, so the arrow from 03 to 04 shows order on the page, not one step leading to the next.

== The downstream half on one slide
layout: flow
step: 07 | Impact | Keys tested within 30 minutes | T1078.004
step: 08 | Impact | The flood | T1496.004
step: 09 | Monetization | A resale market | T1657
Wiz ties both Bedrock cases to one vendor breach it does not name, and could not tie either operation it found to the floods.
notes:
The downstream half: stages 07 to 09, two Amazon Bedrock abuse cases.

Stage 07, under the page's Impact phase, is the first unauthorized activity on the stolen keys, within 30 minutes of the reported credential access. Stage 08, also under Impact, is the flood: about 2.5 million calls across the two keys. Stage 09, under Monetization, is the resale market around them, where two operations Wiz found checked the same keys.

Wiz attributes both cases to one of these vendor breaches and does not name which. It could not tie either operation to the floods. Keep both limits when you present this slide.

The next slides take each stage in turn, starting with stage 01 at Zeabur.

== At Zeabur, an August rotation missed a token
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1552.001 | Unsecured Credentials: Credentials in Files
fact: Token created | January 10, 2024, no expiry
fact: In the repository | January 20, 2026, written in plaintext
fact: August rotation | GitHub App key replaced; repository credentials missed
fact: How obtained | unconfirmed; hypothesis ties it to the August key
Zeabur's October report corrects its own August statement that all internal admin keys had been dealt with.
notes:
Stage 01 is where the platform half starts, and Zeabur's report has an unusually complete paper trail. The page maps this stage to T1552.001, Credentials in Files.

A shared cluster admin token was created on January 10, 2024, with no expiry. On January 20, 2026, it was written in plaintext to a private configuration repository. An August incident then exposed a GitHub App private key, which lets an app authenticate to GitHub as itself. Zeabur replaced that key on August 31 without rotating the credentials in the repository.

Zeabur's October report corrects its own August statement that all internal admin keys had been dealt with.

How the attacker obtained the credentials is unconfirmed. Zeabur's leading hypothesis links them to the August key, and Zeabur says that link remains unproven. Keep that hedge when you tell this: the August key is a hypothesis, not a finding.

== An AWS key and the cluster admin token, used within four minutes
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: 01:36 UTC | October 14: first use of a Zeabur AWS key
fact: 01:40 UTC | first shared cluster access with an admin token
fact: Also recorded | October 10 unauthenticated API access; October 13 failed SSH
Two conditions made that token usable from anywhere: it had no expiry, and the admin interface was reachable from the public internet.
notes:
Stage 02 is where an AWS key and the admin token came into use, on October 14. The page maps it to T1078.004, Cloud Accounts.

The first use of a Zeabur AWS key came at 01:36 UTC on October 14, and the first shared cluster access with an admin token followed at 01:40.

Two conditions made that token usable from anywhere: it had no expiry, and the admin interface was reachable from the public internet.

Zeabur also records unauthenticated API access from a later-abused address on October 10, and failed SSH attempts against three dedicated servers on October 13. Beyond calling the October 10 address later-abused, the page draws nothing more from these, so present them as Zeabur's records.

The page's defense for this stage is to expire admin tokens and hide control planes. It comes back after the stages.

== Secrets across the shared cluster were read
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.007 | Unsecured Credentials: Container API
fact: 12:39 UTC | settings and secrets read, confirmed where read logs exist
fact: 13:40 to 17:04 UTC | commands in 865 database services; not every command succeeded
fact: Scope | 9 regions, partly confirmed
fact: Unknown | what data was taken from those databases
On a shared cluster every customer's API keys live behind one control plane, so one admin credential reaches all of them in a single read.
notes:
Stage 03 is the read across the shared cluster. The page maps it to T1552.007, Container API.

At 12:39 UTC the attacker read the settings and secrets of services on the shared cluster. Zeabur confirms the content was read in regions with complete read logs and cannot confirm the rest. The page sums up the scope as nine regions, partly confirmed.

From 13:40 until 17:04, commands ran inside database containers. Zeabur counts 865 services, 736 of them PostgreSQL databases belonging to n8n deployments. It says not every command succeeded, and that it cannot fully confirm what data was obtained. n8n is a workflow automation tool.

The page's lesson here is concentration. The concentration of secrets is the point of attacking a platform: on a shared cluster every customer's API keys live behind one control plane, so one admin credential reaches all of them in a single read.

The page does not say what software runs the shared cluster, so do not name one.

== Plaintext root passwords opened 101 servers
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1021 | Remote Services
technique: T1578.002 | Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: Create Cloud Instance
fact: Stored in | GCP initialization settings, plaintext root passwords
fact: Read with | an old GCP key, by Zeabur's assessment
fact: Result | root sign-ins to 101 servers, SSH keys on 50, 5 new VMs
fact: In September | found September 3, amid root sign-ins from an unknown source
The weakness was not fixed before October, and Zeabur cannot confirm whether the September activity was the same attacker.
notes:
Stage 04 is a second path, through Google Cloud Platform, or GCP. The page maps it to T1021, Remote Services, and tags T1578.002, Create Cloud Instance.

Root passwords sat in plaintext in GCP dedicated server initialization settings, and Zeabur assesses that the attacker used an old GCP key to read them. Zeabur records root sign-ins to 101 servers belonging to 92 users, attacker SSH keys added to 50 of them, five new virtual machines, and 16 copies of customer disks attached.

There is history here. Zeabur found those root passwords on September 3, during a run of root sign-ins from an unknown source that reached about 98 servers on September 5 alone. The weakness was not fixed before October.

Zeabur cannot confirm whether the September activity was the same attacker, so do not present it as an earlier phase of this attack. Keep the word assesses, too: reading the passwords with an old GCP key is Zeabur's assessment.

== 3,218,965 environment variable records read
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Collection
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
fact: 15:30 UTC | internal network node added
fact: 15:42 UTC | user database backup downloaded by presigned URL
fact: 16:10 to 16:47 UTC | platform database read
fact: Confirmed | 3,218,965 environment variable records, 321,810 accounts
Projects that had moved their variables off Zeabur after August were outside this read.
notes:
Stage 05 is where the two paths met, on October 15. The page maps it to T1213, Data from Information Repositories.

A compromised GCP server was used to add a node to the internal network at 15:30 UTC, which allowed a connection to the platform database. Around the same time, about 53 production secrets were read between 14:52 and 16:51, a backup access key was read at 15:04, and a user database backup was downloaded through a presigned URL at 15:42. The next slide explains presigned URLs in general. The page does not say which storage service held the backup.

The platform database was read between 16:10 and 16:47: 3,218,965 environment variable records and 321,810 accounts, both confirmed. The attacker also called the API as two impersonated accounts, one of them a Zeabur employee's. Zeabur's last observed attacker activity was at 21:05 UTC on October 15.

Projects that had moved their variables off Zeabur after August were outside this read. Zeabur will shut down on December 31, 2026, which it calls an operational decision.

== What a presigned URL is
layout: background
source: Amazon S3 User Guide, presigned URLs | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/using-presigned-url.html
A presigned URL grants time-limited access to an Amazon S3 object without updating the bucket policy, using the credentials of the principal who generated it. AWS calls presigned URLs bearer tokens that grant access to those who possess them.
notes:
Background, from the Amazon S3 User Guide, in general terms. It says nothing about the incident, and the page does not say which storage service held the backup.

AWS describes a presigned URL as a way to grant time-limited access to objects in Amazon S3 without updating the bucket policy. It can be entered in a browser or used by a program to download an object, and it carries the credentials of the AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) principal who generated it.

It can be used multiple times, up to its expiration date and time. In general, it also stops working when the credential used to create it is revoked, deleted or deactivated, even if it was given a later expiration time.

AWS calls presigned URLs bearer tokens that grant access to those who possess them, and recommends protecting them appropriately. Requests made with one can be audited back to the principal or role session that signed it.

== Other platforms: different doors, same prize
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552 | Unsecured Credentials
fact: Railway | from 13:58 UTC October 20, by its estimate
fact: Northflank | first known decryption 02:39 to 03:08 UTC October 30
fact: Undisclosed | Northflank's entry point and customer count
fact: Added by Wiz since | Brev, Trigger.dev, Chainstack, kernel.sh, Blacksmith
The recurring target is stored environment variables, especially keys that grant access to AI models.
notes:
Stage 06 leaves Zeabur for the other platforms. The page maps it to T1552, Unsecured Credentials.

Railway believes its attack ran from 13:58 UTC on October 20 until 03:32 UTC on October 22. By its account, the attacker obtained credentials for its CI, or continuous integration, environment and decrypted a subset of the encrypted environment variables it had queried. Railway says at most 1.85% of workspaces, about 100,000, had some environment variables potentially compromised, and it does not rule out access to user source code. It paused new builds and deployments for all customers and asked GitHub, GitLab, Anthropic, OpenAI, OpenRouter, Stripe and others to revoke potentially compromised keys.

Northflank received an alert from Wiz at 11:43 UTC on October 30 and says it patched the exploit within 60 minutes. The page discloses that Wiz also responded to this incident. Northflank's later updates put the first known decryption at 02:39 to 03:08 UTC that morning. It says customers on dedicated or forward-deployed control planes were unaffected, and it has published neither a root cause nor a count of affected customers.

Trigger.dev says an attacker obtained customer root API keys for some environments. It puts the impact at 0.66% of all customers, withholds the attack vector, and does not connect its incident to the others.

== Different doors, by each platform's account
layout: table
row: Reported | Platform
row: Credential access | Fireworks AI, Zeabur, Lightning AI
row: Privilege escalation in build infrastructure | Railway, which believes so
row: A third-party vendor dependency | Warp, which suggests it
row: A legacy system | OpenCode, which points to it
row: Attempted distillation, as the stated motive | OpenCode, CommandCode and Railway
row: Entry point not disclosed | Northflank
Distillation is training a cheaper model on a stronger model's outputs. Railway's motive comes from its early investigation. The page gives Andromeda no detail.
notes:
This table sets the entry points side by side, each in the platform's own words as the page reports them. Wiz's list shows how little they have in common.

Fireworks AI, Zeabur and Lightning AI cite credential access. Railway believes access came through privilege escalation in its build infrastructure, Warp suggests a third-party vendor dependency, and OpenCode points to a legacy system. OpenCode and CommandCode attribute their incidents to attempted distillation, meaning training a cheaper model on a stronger model's outputs, and Railway's preliminary report says its early investigation points the same way. Northflank has not said how the attacker got in.

The page lists Andromeda among the nine and gives no detail about it.

Since publishing, Wiz has appended five more reported incidents: Brev (NVIDIA), Trigger.dev, Chainstack, kernel.sh and Blacksmith. When it added the first three, it noted that none had shared attack techniques or indicators.

Keep the verbs as each platform uses them. Believes, suggests and points to are not the same as confirms.

== Stolen Bedrock keys validated within 30 minutes, in a one-minute sweep
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Impact
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Delay | within 30 minutes of the reported credential access
fact: First call | one InvokeModel from a residential address
fact: Follow-up | 14 to 120 calls a day from one Go client
fact: Linked to | one vendor breach, not named by Wiz
Low-volume validation like this is easy to lose in normal traffic.
notes:
Stage 07 opens the downstream half. The page maps it to T1078.004, Cloud Accounts, under its Impact phase.

Wiz attributes two Bedrock abuse cases to one of these vendor breaches and does not name which. In both, the first unauthorized activity came within 30 minutes of the reported credential access: a single InvokeModel call from a residential address, in a one-minute sweep across multiple organizations. The call requested Claude Fable 5.1, failed, and fell back to Opus 5.5, which succeeded.

After that, each key saw 14 to 120 calls a day from the same small Go client, with calls on the two keys running about a minute apart. Low-volume validation like this is easy to lose in normal traffic.

Amazon Bedrock is AWS's managed service for using foundation models, and InvokeModel is the Bedrock API call that runs inference on a model with a prompt.

Wiz does not establish a direct link between its listed indicators and the wider Black October incidents.

== Two floods, about 2.5 million Bedrock calls
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Impact
technique: T1496.004 | Resource Hijacking: Cloud Service Hijacking
fact: Case A | about 800,000 calls, about 30 hours, about 90% successful
fact: Case B | about 1.7 million calls, about 13 hours, 1 million+ successful
fact: Enumeration | bulk traffic 22 seconds later, in Case A
fact: Cost | likely over $100,000 at list price, per Wiz
Case B's key was limited to us.* profiles. The restriction cost the abusers a few hours and did not stop them.
notes:
Stage 08 is the flood. The page maps it to T1496.004, Resource Hijacking: Cloud Service Hijacking, and tags it LLMjacking, where someone else's cloud account pays for model use.

In Case A, four days after validation, a rotating proxy pool tried one call per model. A few hours later a boto3 script enumerated Bedrock and EC2 quotas, and bulk traffic began 22 seconds after that. Over about 30 hours the key carried roughly 800,000 calls, about 90% of them successful.

Case B's key was limited to us.* profiles. Seven days after validation it appeared in an apparent reseller pool, and over about 13 hours one Level 3 address made about 1.7 million calls, more than a million of them successful. The next slide sets the two cases side by side.

The same script ran in both cases, down to the version string, and Wiz says the two likely ran up over $100,000 at list price. The restriction cost the abusers a few hours and did not stop them.

boto3 is the AWS software development kit for Python, and EC2 is AWS's virtual server service.

== Case A and Case B, side by side
layout: table
row: Case | Key policy | Before the flood | The flood
row: A | allowed global.* profiles | proxy pool tried one call per model; quota enumeration 22 seconds before bulk traffic | about 800,000 calls over about 30 hours, about 90% successful
row: B | limited to us.* profiles | apparent reseller pool's global.* calls failed; a checker found working model IDs | about 1.7 million calls over about 13 hours, more than a million successful
Case B's restriction caused thousands of failed calls; it cost the abusers a few hours and did not stop them.
notes:
This table sets the two cases side by side, as the page reports them. The same script ran in both, down to the version string.

Case A's key allowed global.* cross-region inference profiles. Four days after validation, a rotating proxy pool tried one call per model. A few hours later a boto3 script enumerated Bedrock and EC2 quotas, and bulk traffic began 22 seconds after that.

Case B's key was limited to us.* profiles. Seven days after validation it appeared in an apparent reseller pool, where thousands of calls failed because the pool requested global.*. A checker then found working model IDs, and the flood followed.

The page draws a defense from this contrast: Case B's restriction caused thousands of failed calls and a detectable delay. Be precise about the result. The restriction cost the abusers a few hours and did not stop them.

The next slide explains what global and geographic inference profiles are, in general terms.

== Geographic and global inference profiles in Amazon Bedrock
layout: background
source: Amazon Bedrock User Guide, cross-Region inference | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/cross-region-inference.html
credit: Also from Geographic and Global cross-Region inference (docs.aws.amazon.com)
An inference profile defines a model and the AWS Regions a request can be routed to. Geographic profiles, with prefixes such as us, keep requests within one geography. Global profiles route them to supported commercial Regions worldwide.
notes:
Background, from the Amazon Bedrock User Guide, in general terms. It says nothing about the incident.

AWS says inference profiles define a foundation model and the AWS Regions to which requests can be routed. Cross-Region inference works through them, routing requests by using compute across different AWS Regions.

There are two kinds of cross-Region profile. A geographic profile is tied to a geography such as the US, the EU or APAC (Asia Pacific), and requests to it stay within that geography. These profiles carry geographic prefixes such as us, eu, apac and in. A global profile routes requests to supported commercial AWS Regions worldwide, and AWS's example policy names it as global followed by the model name.

To let an IAM user or role invoke a geographic profile, AWS says to allow access to the profile itself, to the foundation model in the source Region, and to the foundation model in every destination Region the profile lists.

Also from Geographic cross-Region inference: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/geographic-cross-region-inference.html

And from Global cross-Region inference: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/global-cross-region-inference.html

== Infrastructure tied to resellers checked the same stolen keys
layout: stage
stage: 09
phase: Monetization
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: momo | 23.106.46.239, had access to both keys
fact: Xingjiabi | 38.246.114.118, enumerated the Case A key
fact: Attempted | PutAccountDataRetention with the Case B key
fact: Limit | neither operator tied to the floods by Wiz
Wiz reads their checks as evidence of an active resale market.
notes:
Stage 09 is the market around the stolen keys. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft, under its Monetization phase.

Wiz found two operations touching these keys. One, which it calls momo, had access to both keys, and its infrastructure includes a site advertising discounted frontier-model access for USDT, the Tether stablecoin. The same address hosted a console titled ARTEX on October 8, the name of the framework on the Storm-0940 and VAPOR PANDA pages, though Wiz does not connect them. Zeabur later named that address in its own compromise, and the page says that overlap does not show Zeabur's keys are the ones in these cases.

The other operation, Xingjiabi, enumerated the Case A key and tested the Case B key after the abuse had started.

Wiz could not tie either operator to the floods. It reads their checks as evidence of an active resale market, and one snapshot showed a page scoring users for distillation behavior, possibly to avoid account suspensions.

== A resale market, per Wiz and Google
layout: compare
col: Wiz, on these keys | Two operations, momo and Xingjiabi, checked the stolen keys | One snapshot showed a page scoring users for distillation behavior | Wiz could not tie either operator to the floods
col: Google's research, separately | Calls such resellers LLM mixers: gateways that proxy, pool and resell unauthorized model access | In one case, more than 500,000 calls in under eight hours on stolen keys | Published November 2; does not mention these platforms
Google acquired Wiz in March 2026, as the page discloses.
notes:
Two pieces of research describe the market these keys went into, and the page keeps them apart.

Wiz found the two operations on the previous slide, reads their checks as evidence of an active resale market, and could not tie either operator to the floods.

Google's threat intelligence group describes the same kind of business in separate research published on November 2, which does not mention these platforms. It calls the resellers LLM mixers: gateways that proxy, pool and resell unauthorized model access. In one case it describes, an actor made more than 500,000 calls in under eight hours on stolen third-party keys to power a token routing service called Jet Night. Jet Night offered an unlimited plan for about $60 a month and passed nearly $1 million in token costs to the victim organization. Google believes the service relies on stolen keys to stay profitable at that price.

As the page discloses, Google acquired Wiz in March 2026. LLM stands for large language model.

== Defenses against stages 01 to 06
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Assume a platform's secret store is as exposed as the platform | -
row: Rotate everything a leaked path could reach | 01
row: Expire admin tokens and hide control planes | 01 | 02
row: Keep secrets out of instance metadata and startup settings | 04
No defense on the page is tied to stage 03, 05 or 06. The first is stated for hosted platforms in general.
notes:
This matrix and the next set the page's six defenses against its nine stages, split at the page's two halves. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words tie it to that stage.

Rotating everything a leaked path could reach cites Zeabur's August rotation, which replaced the key it knew about and left plaintext credentials in the repository: stage 01. Expiring admin tokens and hiding control planes cites a cluster admin token created in 2024 with no expiry, stage 01's token, which still worked in 2026 from the public internet, as in stage 02. Keeping secrets out of instance metadata and startup settings cites root passwords in initialization settings: stage 04.

The first defense, assuming a platform's secret store is as exposed as the platform, is stated for hosted platforms in general and cites no stage, so its row is marked as naming none.

No defense on the page is tied to stages 03, 05 or 06. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end.

== Defenses against stages 07 to 09
layout: matrix
columns: 07 | 08 | 09
row: Scope AI keys by model, region and budget | 08
row: Treat first use as the alert | 07 | 08
No defense on the page is tied to stage 09, the resale market.
notes:
The downstream half has two defenses tied to its stages.

Scoping AI keys by model, region and budget cites Case B's restriction to us.* profiles, which caused thousands of failed calls and a detectable delay: stage 08.

Treating first use as the alert names InvokeModel from a new network; stage 07's first unauthorized activity was a single InvokeModel call from a residential address. The same defense names catalog probing with one call per model, and quota enumeration followed by bulk traffic seconds later, which matches the run-up to Case A's flood in stage 08.

No defense on the page is tied to stage 09, the resale market. That gap is part of the second discussion question.

== Store as exposed as the platform; rotate what a leak reaches
layout: compare
col: Assume the store is as exposed as the platform | Whoever controls a hosted platform can read its environment variables | Use workload identity or OIDC in place of a static key, where supported | Then a bulk read returns nothing long-lived
col: Rotate what a leaked path could reach (stage 01) | Zeabur's August rotation replaced the key it knew about | It left plaintext credentials in the repository that key could read | List what the compromised identity could reach, then rotate all of it
A statement that all keys were rotated should come from a checked inventory.
notes:
Two defenses come from the platform half. The page notes that Wiz's report offers no recommendations section, so its list reflects the failures in the record.

First, assume a platform's secret store is as exposed as the platform. Environment variables on a hosted platform are readable by whoever controls that platform. Where a provider supports workload identity or OIDC, use that in place of a static key, so that a bulk read returns nothing long-lived. OIDC is OpenID Connect, an identity standard. The page ties this defense to no single stage.

Second, rotate everything a leaked path could reach. Zeabur's August rotation replaced the key it knew about and left plaintext credentials in the repository that key could read. After any incident, list what the compromised identity could reach, then rotate all of it. A statement that all keys were rotated should come from a checked inventory. Stage 01 shows why: Zeabur's October report corrects its own August statement that all internal admin keys had been dealt with.

== Expire admin tokens, and keep secrets out of instance settings
layout: compare
col: Expire tokens, hide control planes (stages 01, 02) | A no-expiry 2024 admin token still worked in 2026 from the public internet | Use short-lived credentials for cluster administration | Restrict the API server to a management network
col: Secrets out of instance settings (stage 04) | Root passwords in initialization settings were readable by any key with access to them | Use OS Login or short-lived SSH certificates | Scan instance metadata for credentials as you scan repositories
notes:
Two more defenses each answer specific stages.

Expire admin tokens and hide control planes. A cluster admin token created in 2024 with no expiry still worked in 2026, from the public internet. That is stage 01's token and stage 02's admin interface. Use short-lived credentials for cluster administration, and restrict the API server to a management network.

Keep secrets out of instance metadata and startup settings. Root passwords in initialization settings were readable by any key with access to those settings, which is stage 04. Use OS Login or short-lived SSH certificates, and scan instance metadata for credentials the same way you scan repositories.

OS Login is Google Cloud's way of managing SSH access to virtual machines through IAM permissions, without creating and managing individual SSH keys.

== Scope AI keys; treat first use as the alert
layout: compare
col: Scope AI keys | By model, region and budget | Case B's us.* restriction caused thousands of failed calls | Budget alerts and quotas low enough that abuse hits a ceiling | One key per workload, so one theft does not spread
col: Treat first use as the alert | InvokeModel from a new network | Catalog probing, one call per model | Quota enumeration, particularly when bulk traffic follows | Billing reviewed as a security signal
notes:
The last two defenses come from the downstream half.

Scope AI keys by model, region and budget. Case B's restriction to us.* profiles caused thousands of failed calls and a detectable delay. Set budget alerts and quotas low enough that abuse hits a ceiling, and give each workload its own key so that one theft does not spread. Northflank now also asks customers to turn on IP restrictions and budget limits at their providers wherever those exist.

Treat first use as the alert. Look for InvokeModel from a new network, catalog probing with one call per model, and quota enumeration, particularly when bulk traffic follows seconds later. Google's separate research adds validation sweeps of single-token requests, max_tokens=1, and says any call that disables Bedrock model invocation logging from a principal other than infrastructure-as-code should be treated as high severity. Review billing as a security signal too.

If you used any of the named platforms, the page says to rotate stored keys now and check provider usage logs.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Could a bulk read of your hosted platform's environment variables return a long-lived key?
check: After your last incident, did you rotate everything the compromised identity could reach?
check: Does any cluster admin token you hold have no expiry?
check: Can the public internet reach your cluster's API server?
check: Do instance metadata or startup settings hold passwords or keys?
check: Is each AI key scoped by model, region and budget, one per workload?
check: Would InvokeModel from a new network, or quota enumeration, raise an alert?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether a bulk read of the environment variables you keep on a hosted platform would return anything long-lived, and whether workload identity or OIDC could replace those static keys. Ask whether, after your last incident, you rotated everything the compromised identity could reach, working from a checked inventory.

Ask whether any cluster admin token has no expiry, and whether the API server is restricted to a management network. Ask whether instance metadata or startup settings hold credentials, and whether you scan them the way you scan repositories.

Ask whether each AI key is scoped by model, region and budget, with its own key per workload, and whether anything would alert on InvokeModel from a new network, on catalog probing or quota enumeration, or on billing.

If you used any of the platforms named in this deck, the page says to rotate stored keys now and check provider usage logs.

== Case B's key was scoped. Did that help?
layout: question
Case B's key was limited to us.* profiles, and the apparent reseller pool requested global.* profiles.

What did the restriction achieve, and what would you pair it with?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page gives the facts. Case B's key was limited to us.* profiles. Seven days after validation it appeared in an apparent reseller pool, where thousands of calls failed because the pool requested global.*. A checker then found working model IDs, and over about 13 hours one address made about 1.7 million calls.

Ask each person what the restriction bought, and what they would add so that it does more than delay the abuse. The answer slide gives what the page says.

== What the page says about scoping keys
layout: answer
The restriction caused thousands of failed calls and a detectable delay, and did not stop the abuse.
- Budget alerts and quotas low enough that abuse hits a ceiling.
- One key per workload, so one theft does not spread.
notes:
The page's answer: the restriction slowed the abuse and did not stop it.

Case B's restriction to us.* profiles caused thousands of failed calls and a detectable delay. The restriction cost the abusers a few hours and did not stop them.

The same defense, scoping AI keys by model, region and budget, adds two more controls: budget alerts and quotas low enough that abuse hits a ceiling, and a separate key for each workload so that one theft does not spread.

Northflank now also asks customers to turn on IP restrictions and budget limits at their providers wherever those exist. Bring the group's ideas back to these.

== What would you add for stages 03, 05, 06 and 09?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to the cluster read, the platform database read, the other platforms' incidents, or the resale market.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the two defense matrices.

The page ties defenses to stages 01, 02, 04, 07 and 08. Its first defense, assuming a platform's secret store is as exposed as the platform, is stated in general and names no stage. Nothing on the page is tied to stage 03, where secrets across the shared cluster were read; stage 05, where the platform database was read; stage 06, the other platforms' incidents; or stage 09, the resale market.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to these four stages
layout: answer
The page ties no defense to them; its first defense names no stage at all. Any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against reported details, like the 12:39 UTC secrets read or the added network node.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stages 03, 05, 06 or 09, and this deck adds none of its own. The first defense names no stage, so the group may argue it reaches the cluster and database reads; that argument is the group's own.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each against the facts the page reports.

For stage 03, those facts are the 12:39 UTC read of service settings and secrets, and commands in 865 database services. For stage 05, they are the internal network node added at 15:30 UTC, the backup downloaded through a presigned URL, and the platform database read between 16:10 and 16:47. For stage 06, they are the different entry points each platform reports. For stage 09, they are momo's and Xingjiabi's checks on the stolen keys, and the attempted PutAccountDataRetention call.

